{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdXmcnzcwRYtE7dCuSnD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The first real-time AI detection of a pulsar, a rapidly spinning stellar remnant that pulses like a cosmic lighthouse, was made in the Crab Nebula using the pipeline now running at the Allen Telescope Array. NVIDIA's article treats it as proof the pipeline works, and the reason it matters comes from a constraint the article describes: scientists need to identify potentially interesting signals as observations unfold, and radio telescopes generate enormous volumes of data.\n\nDetecting a signal after the fact means finding it in stored data once the observation is over. Detecting it in real time means the finding is available while the telescope is still pointed at the source. The article also says agents at the array can steer the antennae and calibrate and focus the instrument, so my inference is that a detection and a response to it could happen within the same observation. The article does not describe that loop explicitly, and the Crab result involves a known object, so it shows the detection half of the process working.\n\nThe speed rests on how the data is handled. DAQIRI feeds terabits per second into GPU memory so processing happens in flight, and the system is described as handling 2.4 terabits per second on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell GPUs. The Crab detection is the single outcome cited for all of it, and what the same setup finds in unfamiliar sources is still to be shown."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1210,"cache_read_input_tokens":6891,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1210},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_to